.TH "DOG" "1" "" "dog 0.8.5" "DNS Client"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
dog \-\- a command\-line DNS client

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
`\f[CR]dog [OPTIONS] [\-\-] <arguments>\fP`
.PP
\fBdog\fP is a command\-line DNS client\.
It has colourful output, supports the DNS\-over\-TLS and DNS\-over\-HTTPS protocols, and can emit JSON\.

.SH "EXAMPLES"
\fB`\f[CR]dog example\.net\fP`\fP
.RS
Query the `\f[CR]A\fP` and `\f[CR]AAAA\fP` records of a domain using default settings
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog example\.net MX\fP`\fP
.RS
\.\.\.looking up `\f[CR]MX\fP` records instead
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog example\.net MX @1\.1\.1\.1\fP`\fP
.RS
\.\.\.using a specific nameserver instead
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog example\.net MX @1\.1\.1\.1 \-T\fP`\fP
.RS
\.\.\.using TCP rather than UDP
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog \-q example\.net \-t MX \-n 1\.1\.1\.1 \-T\fP`\fP
.RS
As above, but using explicit arguments
.RE

.SH "QUERY OPTIONS"
\fB`\f[CR]\-q\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-query <HOST>\fP`\fP
.RS
Host name or domain name to query\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-t\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-type <TYPE>\fP`\fP
.RS
Type of the DNS record being queried\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-n\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-nameserver <ADDR>\fP`\fP
.RS
Address of the nameserver to send packets to\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-class <CLASS>\fP`\fP
.RS
Network class of the DNS record being queried (`\f[CR]IN\fP`, `\f[CR]CH\fP`, `\f[CR]HS\fP`)
.RE
.PP
By default, dog will request A and AAAA records using the system default resolver\. A missing address family is left out when the other one answered; dog reports an error only when neither query returned a record\. At least one domain name must be passed \-\- dog will not automatically query the root nameservers\.
.PP
Query options passed in using a command\-line option, such as '`\f[CR]\-\-query lookup\.dog\fP`' or '`\f[CR]\-\-type MX\fP`', or as plain arguments, such as '`\f[CR]lookup\.dog\fP`' or '`\f[CR]MX\fP`'\. dog will make an intelligent guess as to what plain arguments mean (`\f[CR]MX\fP` is quite clearly a type), which makes it easier to compose ad\-hoc queries quickly\. If precision is desired, use the long\-form options\.
.PP
If more than one domain, type, nameserver, or class is specified, dog will perform one query for each combination, and display the combined results in a table\. For example, passing three type arguments and two domain name arguments will send six requests\.
.PP
DNS traditionally uses port 53 for both TCP and UDP\. To use a resolver with a different port, include the port number after a colon (`\f[CR]:\fP`) in the nameserver address\.

.SH "SENDING OPTIONS"
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-edns <SETTING>\fP`\fP
.RS
Whether to OPT in to EDNS (disable, hide, show)\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-txid <NUMBER>\fP`\fP
.RS
Set the transaction ID to a specific value\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-Z <TWEAKS>\fP`\fP
.RS
Set uncommon protocol tweaks: `\f[CR]aa\fP` (`\f[CR]authoritative\fP`) sets the AA header bit; `\f[CR]ad\fP` (`\f[CR]authentic\fP`) sets the AD header bit; `\f[CR]cd\fP` (`\f[CR]checking\-disabled\fP`) sets the CD header bit; `\f[CR]do\fP` (`\f[CR]dnssec\-ok\fP`) sets the DNSSEC OK bit in the EDNS OPT record; `\f[CR]bufsize=<NUMBER>\fP` sets the EDNS UDP payload size\.
.RE

.SH "PROTOCOL OPTIONS"
\fB`\f[CR]\-U\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-udp\fP`\fP
.RS
Use the DNS protocol over UDP\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-T\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-tcp\fP`\fP
.RS
Use the DNS protocol over TCP\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-S\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-tls\fP`\fP
.RS
Use the DNS\-over\-TLS protocol\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-H\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-https\fP`\fP
.RS
Use the DNS\-over\-HTTPS protocol\.
.RE
.PP
By default, dog will use the UDP protocol, automatically re\-sending the request using TCP if the response indicates that the message is too large for UDP\. Passing `\f[CR]\-\-udp\fP` will only use UDP and will fail in this case; passing `\f[CR]\-\-tcp\fP` will use TCP by default\.
.PP
The DNS\-over\-TLS (DoT) and DNS\-over\-HTTPS (DoH) protocols are available with the `\f[CR]\-\-tls\fP` and `\f[CR]\-\-https\fP` options\. Bear in mind that the system default resolver is unlikely to respond to requests using these protocols\.
.PP
Note that if a hostname or domain name is given as a nameserver, rather than an IP address, the resolution of that host is performed by the operating system, \fInot\fP by dog\.
.PP
The HTTPS transport takes the nameserver the same way as the others, as an address or a host name, not a URL: dog sends its queries to `\f[CR]https://<nameserver>/dns\-query\fP`, the path public DoH resolvers use\. A host name's addresses do not always serve every protocol: `\f[CR]cloudflare\-dns\.com\fP` answers DoH but not plain DNS or DoT\.

.SH "OUTPUT OPTIONS"
\fB`\f[CR]\-1\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-short\fP`\fP
.RS
Short mode: display only the data of each answer, one per line\. The exit status is 2 if the server gave no result\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-J\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-json\fP`\fP
.RS
Display the output as JSON\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-color\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-colour <WHEN>\fP`\fP
.RS
When to colourise the output\. This can be '`\f[CR]always\fP`' or '`\f[CR]automatic\fP`', or '`\f[CR]never\fP`'\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-seconds\fP`\fP
.RS
Do not format durations as hours and minutes; instead, display them as seconds\.
.RE

.SH "META OPTIONS"
\fB`\f[CR]\-V\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-version\fP`\fP
.RS
Displays the version of dog being invoked\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-?\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-help\fP`\fP
.RS
Displays an overview of the command\-line options\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-l\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-list\fP`\fP
.RS
List known DNS record types\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-v\fP`, `\f[CR]\-\-verbose\fP`\fP
.RS
Print verbose information\.
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]\-\-completions <SHELL>\fP`\fP
.RS
Generate shell completions\.
.RE

.SH "SHORTCUTS"
.PP
Instead of using the `\f[CR]\-q\fP`, `\f[CR]\-t\fP`, and `\f[CR]\-n\fP` flags, you can provide the arguments directly:
\fB`\f[CR]dog lookup\.dog\fP`\fP
.RS
Query a domain for its A and AAAA records
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog lookup\.dog MX\fP`\fP
.RS
Query a domain for a specific type
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog lookup\.dog @8\.8\.8\.8\fP`\fP
.RS
Query a domain using a specific nameserver
.RE
\fB`\f[CR]dog 1\.1\.1\.1\fP`\fP
.RS
Perform a reverse lookup for an IP address
.RE

.SH "RECORD TYPES"
.PP
dog supports the following record types: `\f[CR]A\fP`, `\f[CR]AAAA\fP`, `\f[CR]ANAME\fP`, `\f[CR]ANY\fP`, `\f[CR]AXFR\fP`, `\f[CR]CAA\fP`, `\f[CR]CDNSKEY\fP`, `\f[CR]CDS\fP`, `\f[CR]CNAME\fP`, `\f[CR]CSYNC\fP`, `\f[CR]DNSKEY\fP`, `\f[CR]DS\fP`, `\f[CR]HINFO\fP`, `\f[CR]HTTPS\fP`, `\f[CR]IXFR\fP`, `\f[CR]KEY\fP`, `\f[CR]MX\fP`, `\f[CR]NAPTR\fP`, `\f[CR]NS\fP`, `\f[CR]NSEC\fP`, `\f[CR]NSEC3\fP`, `\f[CR]NSEC3PARAM\fP`, `\f[CR]NULL\fP`, `\f[CR]OPENPGPKEY\fP`, `\f[CR]OPT\fP`, `\f[CR]PTR\fP`, `\f[CR]RRSIG\fP`, `\f[CR]SIG\fP`, `\f[CR]SOA\fP`, `\f[CR]SRV\fP`, `\f[CR]SSHFP\fP`, `\f[CR]SVCB\fP`, `\f[CR]TLSA\fP`, `\f[CR]TSIG\fP`, `\f[CR]TXT\fP`\.

.SH "ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES"
.PP
dog responds to the following environment variables:

.SS "`\f[CR]DOG_DEBUG\fP`"
.PP
Set this to any non\-empty value to have dog emit debugging information to standard error\. For more in\-depth output, set this to the exact string '`\f[CR]trace\fP`'\.

.SH "EXIT STATUSES"
\fB0\fP
.RS
If everything goes OK\.
.RE
\fB1\fP
.RS
If there was a network, I/O, or TLS error during operation\.
.RE
\fB2\fP
.RS
If there is no result from the server when running in short mode\. This can be any received server error, not just NXDOMAIN\.
.RE
\fB3\fP
.RS
If there was a problem with the command\-line arguments\.
.RE
.PP
A closed output pipe, as in `\f[CR]dog example\.com | head \-n 1\fP`, is not an error: dog stops quietly with status 0\.

